    "result_text": "The change is implemented and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe feature was partially scaffolded \u2014 `sharesHousehold` already existed on the request DTO and the household-conflict check already let opted-in requests through. What was missing was the actual assignment and return of a shared `householdId`. I added:\n\n**Persistence / model**\n- `Owner.java` \u2014 new `householdId` field (`@Column(name = \"household_id\")`) with getter/setter.\n- `household_id` column added to all four schema dialects (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`). Tests run on the `hsqldb` profile.\n\n**API contract**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added a read-only `householdId` property to the `Owner` response schema. The build regenerates `OwnerDto` from this, and MapStruct auto-maps it by name, so `GET /api/owners/{id}` now returns it.\n\n**Request pipeline** (`officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`)\n- Inserted a new `assignHousehold` step between `build` and `assignCustomerCode` (under the same `transaction` governance), backed by a new function class `AssignHousehold`.\n\n**`AssignHousehold` logic** \u2014 when `sharesHousehold` is true, it finds existing owners with the same normalized last name + address, then:\n- reuses their `householdId` if one exists, otherwise derives a stable id deterministically from the household key (`HH-` + SHA-256 prefix);\n- assigns that id to the new owner **and** back-fills it onto existing housemates (saving them), so every member shares one identifier.\n\nNon-opted-in requests are untouched (`householdId` stays null; the existing 409 conflict behavior is unchanged).\n\n**Verification**\n- `./mvnw -o test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `AcceptanceTest.coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` passes.\n\nOne honest caveat: `./mvnw verify` fails on the JaCoCo coverage gate (0.14 vs. the 0.85/0.66 minimums). That is a **pre-existing structural condition** \u2014 the project has a single acceptance test covering the whole app, so the gate can't be met by any small feature change; it's not a regression from this work (my new code's branches are actually exercised by the test). I recorded this in memory so it isn't misread later.",
